<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saddam Hussein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saddam Hussein was the Ba'athist ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, effective leader from the 1970s and president from 1979 to 2003. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> relied on the security services and repression, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anfal-campaign">Anfal campaign</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>. He led Iraq into war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> (1980–1988) and the invasion of Kuwait in 1990. He was overthrown by the US-led invasion of 2003 and executed in 2006.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein.jpg" alt="Saddam Hussein" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iraqi News Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saddam_Hussein_in_1998.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saudi Arabia and Kuwait spent $25 billion backing <b>Saddam Hussein</b> in the Iran–Iraq War of 1980–1988, yet after Iraq's invasion of Kuwait in 1990 King Fahd invited American and coalition troops onto Saudi soil, and in 1991 Saudi forces flew bombing raids and joined the land invasion that…</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…finds personalist and military dictatorships more likely than democracies to start wars, since no voters or civilian elite can call their rulers to account, as <b>Saddam Hussein</b>’s invasions of Iran in 1980 and Kuwait in 1990 and the Argentine junta’s invasion of the Falkland Islands in 1982 illustrate.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>My Homeland, a poem by the Palestinian Ibrahim Tuqan of about 1934, served for much of the century as an unofficial anthem of the Palestinians, and in 2004 became the anthem of Iraq, after the anthem associated with <b>Saddam Hussein</b>'s government had been set aside.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ba'athist government was overthrown in 2003, that older piece was brought back for a short time in place of &quot;Arḍulfurātayni&quot;, the anthem in use since 1981 and associated with <b>Saddam Hussein</b>'s government, until Tuqan's song replaced it in 2004.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mawtini">My Homeland</a></li><li><blockquote>As US troops entered central Baghdad, a statue of <b>Saddam Hussein</b> in Firdos Square was toppled with the help of a Marine vehicle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Saddam Hussein</b>'s forces overran Kuwait in a matter of hours.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
